# Example environment for the Vedanta inventory dashboard POC.
# Copy to .env if you want to keep values in a file; none are required for the
# default `npm ci && npm run build && npm run preview` flow. No secrets here.
#
# This list was produced by searching the repository for every environment read
# (process.env, import.meta.env, os.environ). The dashboard is a static app that
# reads only public/data/*.json at runtime, so it needs no API keys or database
# credentials. Vite loads .env files itself, but only VITE_-prefixed variables
# reach browser code and the app defines none.

# --- Build time (vite.config.ts) -------------------------------------------

# Public URL path the built site is served from. Leave unset (defaults to "/")
# when the app is served at the root of a host. Set it when publishing under a
# sub-path, e.g. GitHub Pages at https://<owner>.github.io/<repo>/ needs
# "/<repo>/". Must start and end with a slash. The data loader
# (src/data/loader.ts) builds its JSON URLs from this value.
# Read from the shell environment of the build command, e.g.
#   PAGES_BASE=/Vedanta-Inventory-Analytics/ npm run build
PAGES_BASE=/

# --- Running the preview server --------------------------------------------

# Port for `vite preview`. Vite does not read this variable on its own; it is
# passed through the command line, as in the cohort preview command:
#   npx vite preview --host 0.0.0.0 --port "$PORT" --strictPort
# The Claire sandbox (sandbox_serve) assigns it. Locally, pick any free port;
# if you omit --port, vite preview uses its own default (4173).
PORT=4173

# --- Not environment variables ---------------------------------------------
# The data refresh scripts (scripts/build_data.py, scripts/build_forecast.py)
# read no environment variables. They take paths as arguments:
#   python scripts/build_data.py <csv-dir> <source-metadata.json>
#   python scripts/build_forecast.py <download-dir> <experiment-metadata.json>
# The SAP HANA and Context Flow credentials used to produce those inputs are
# held in the Context Flow connection (Integrations), never in this repository.
